{"name":"Grotesque Green","hex":"#64e986","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grotesque","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Distorted and unnatural in shape or size; abnormal, especially in a hideous way.","Disgusting or otherwise viscerally revolting."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A style of ornamentation characterized by fanciful combinations of intertwined forms.","Anything grotesque."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grotesque"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grotesque","blocks":[{"text":"Grotesque is an adjective often used to describe weird shapes and distorted forms such as Halloween masks. In art, performance, and literature, however, grotesque may also refer to something that simultaneously invokes an audience feeling of uncomfortable bizarreness as well as sympathetic pity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Michelangelo_Buonarroti_-_Studies_-_WGA15523.jpg/330px-Michelangelo_Buonarroti_-_Studies_-_WGA15523.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grotesque","wikidata":"Q218248"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Domus_fresco.jpg/330px-Domus_fresco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Domus_fresco.jpg/330px-Domus_fresco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman frescos in Nero's Domus Aurea, Rome, unknown painter, c. 64–68 AD.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADomus_fresco.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Biblioteca_Duomo_Siena-2_Apr_2008.jpg/500px-Biblioteca_Duomo_Siena-2_Apr_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Biblioteca_Duomo_Siena-2_Apr_2008.jpg/1280px-Biblioteca_Duomo_Siena-2_Apr_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Biblioteca_Duomo_Siena-2_Apr_2008.jpg/500px-Biblioteca_Duomo_Siena-2_Apr_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ceiling of the Piccolomini Library, Siena Cathedral, S